            "text/markdown": "# Professional Resume Review & Optimization Report\n\n---\n\n## 1. Resume Score: 38/100\n\n### Score Breakdown:\n*   **Formatting & Parsibility:** 10/30 (Severe text fragmentation and spacing issues)\n*   **Content & Impact:** 12/30 (Projects lack metrics; experiences are absent)\n*   **Skills & Keywords:** 10/20 (Very few tech skills listed; modern libraries missing)\n*   **Professionalism:** 6/20 (Includes obsolete elements like \"Declaration\", \"Parents' Names\", and \"Marital Status\")\n\n---\n\n## 2. ATS Score Analysis (Current: 21.4%)\n\nThe ATS score is critically low primarily due to **formatting glitches** and **unparseable text blocks**. \n*   **Word-Splitting Errors (The \"Spacing\" Issue):** The resume is filled with random spaces inside words (e.g., `o rganization’ s`, `resp onsibility`, `tec hnolog y`, `p redictive`). An ATS parser reads `tec hnolog y` as three separate gibberish words rather than the keyword **\"technology\"**. This is why only 3 skills (`SQL`, `Pandas`, `NumPy`) were extracted out of your entire resume.\n*   **Table Formatting:** The education section uses a table format. While some modern ATS can parse tables, older systems fail, turning your academic credentials into unreadable text.\n*   **Irrelevant Sections:** Sections like \"Declaration\" and \"Personal Details\" distract the parser and dilute the keyword density of the document.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Missing Skills\n\nTo make your resume competitive for Software Engineering and Data Science roles, you must bridge the gap between basic classroom knowledge and industry-standard tools.\n\n### Key Missing Technical Skills:\n*   **Version Control:** Git, GitHub\n*   **Advanced Python/ML Libraries:** TensorFlow, Keras, Scikit-Learn (explicitly in the skills section, not just projects)\n*   **Data Structures & Algorithms (DSA):** Essential for a CSE student.\n*   **Modern Web Tech:** JavaScript (highly recommended alongside HTML/CSS/Bootstrap), React.js (optional but beneficial)\n*   **Development Tools:** VS Code, Jupyter Notebook\n\n---\n\n## 4. Grammar & Formatting Suggestions\n\nThe system flagged **108 grammar errors**, almost all of which are due to extreme word-splitting/typesetting issues. \n\n### Critical Text Corrections:\n*   **Correction 1 (Career Objective):** \n    *   *Current:* \"To strive to w ard s achieving the o rganization’ s go als...\"\n    *   *Correction:* \"To strive toward achieving the organization's goals...\"\n*   **Correction 2 (Education):** \n    *   *Current:* \"G ATES Institute o f tec hnolog y AUTO NO M O US\"\n    *   *Correction:* \"GATES Institute of Technology (Autonomous)\"\n*   **Correction 3 (Project):** \n    *   *Current:* \"Developed a p redictive m o del to fo recast sto ck m arket...\"\n    *   *Correction:* \"Developed a predictive model to forecast stock market...\"\n\n### Formatting Changes:\n1.  **Remove Personal Details:** Delete *Father's Name, Mother's Name, Marital Status*, and the *Declaration*. Modern professional resumes do not contain this information as it can trigger bias and wastes valuable space.\n2.  **Fix the Layout:** Rebuild the resume in a clean, single-column LaTeX format or a standard Google Docs/Microsoft Word template without tables. Ensure there are no double-spaces or broken words.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Project Improvements\n\nYour **Stock Price Prediction System** project has a solid foundation, but it needs to show *results* and *impact*.\n\n*   **Add Quantifiable Metrics:** Instead of just saying you built it, specify its performance. What was the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) or RMSE of your LSTM model?\n    *   *Example:* \"...resulting in a directional accuracy of **84%** and reducing mean squared error (MSE) by **12%** compared to standard baseline models.\"\n*   **Include Git Links:** Always add a hyperlink to the GitHub repository of your projects so recruiters can review your code.\n*   **Add a Second Project:** As a CSE student graduating in 2027, you have time. Add at least one more project (e.g., a Full-Stack Web Application or a DBMS-based SQL project) to show versatility.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Recommended Resume Summary\n*Replace your generic \"Career Objective\" with this modern, impact-driven Professional Summary:*\n\n> \"Detail-oriented Computer Science & Engineering undergraduate (Class of 2027) with a strong foundation in Python, SQL, and frontend web technologies. Proven ability to implement machine learning pipelines, as demonstrated by building predictive time-series models (LSTM). Eager to leverage technical skills in software engineering and data analytics internships.\"\n\n---\n\n## 7. Best Job Roles\n\nGiven your current trajectory (CSE, Python, SQL, ML basics), you are best suited for:\n1.  **Software Engineer Intern**\n2.  **Data Analyst Intern**\n3.  **Python Developer Intern**\n4.  **Frontend Web Developer Intern**\n\n---\n\n## 8. Interview Tips\n\n1.  **Master the Math Behind the Model:** If you put \"LSTM\" (Long Short-Term Memory) on your resume, interviewers will ask you *why* you chose it over standard RNNs or ARIMA models. Be ready to explain vanishing gradients.\n2.  **Practice SQL Queries:** You will almost certainly be asked to write SQL queries. Master `JOINs`, `GROUP BY`, `HAVING`, and window functions.\n3.  **Brush Up on OOPs Concepts:** For Java and Python, make sure you can explain Object-Oriented Programming principles (Inheritance, Polymorphism, Encapsulation, Abstraction) with real-world examples.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Final Verdict\n\n### **Verdict: Needs Urgent Revision**\n\nYour academic track record is excellent (with high scores like 94% in SSC, 88% in Intermediate, and 82% in B.Tech). However, **your resume is severely holding you back.** The text corruption/spacing issues render this document completely invisible to modern ATS screening systems.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n1. Re-type the entire resume in a standard word processor (like Google Docs) to eliminate the split-word encoding errors.\n2. Structure your skills clearly without tables.\n3. Remove the obsolete personal details and declaration.\n4. Add GitHub links to your projects."
